{"conjecture": "The reliability of counterexample-guided inductive synthesis (CEGIS) in producing programs that provably satisfy their abstract specifications can be transformed from an open-ended problem into a decidable one by constraining the search space to a specific class of program sketches with bounded non-determinism. Specifically, if the sketch grammar restricts control flow to a fixed loop bound and limits holes to polynomial templates, a PRA-style verifier can partition the sketch space into equivalence classes where any synthesized program either universally satisfies the specification or admits a compact, symbolic counterexample derivable from the template structure itself. This structural restriction guarantees that the CEGIS loop will converge within a number of iterations bounded by the number of distinct abstract interpretations of the sketch, rather than the size of the input domain."}
